1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,284
Watch a heart monitor in a biology video.

2
00:00:03,284 --> 00:00:04,184
Beep.

3
00:00:03,855 --> 00:00:04,755
Beep.

4
00:00:04,426 --> 00:00:05,326
Beep.

5
00:00:05,397 --> 00:00:09,163
The line draws a spike, then
the same spike again, and again.

6
00:00:09,163 --> 00:00:10,948
The same shape, every beat.

7
00:00:11,348 --> 00:00:15,336
Diya saw this while studying at her
desk, and thought of the sine wave.

8
00:00:15,686 --> 00:00:16,618
Both repeat.

9
00:00:16,618 --> 00:00:20,992
But how long is one repeat of sine,
and why does it repeat at all?

10
00:00:21,492 --> 00:00:25,870
Today, periodicity, and the hidden
mirror symmetry of the trigonometric

11
00:00:25,870 --> 00:00:26,770
graphs.

12
00:00:27,177 --> 00:00:29,637
Turn P once round the unit circle.

13
00:00:29,637 --> 00:00:33,749
That is two pi radians, or
three hundred and sixty degrees.

14
00:00:34,149 --> 00:00:35,382
Now keep turning.

15
00:00:35,382 --> 00:00:39,727
Thirty degrees, then thirty plus
three sixty, which is three ninety.

16
00:00:40,127 --> 00:00:42,726
P lands on exactly the same point.

17
00:00:42,726 --> 00:00:44,634
Same height, same side.

18
00:00:44,634 --> 00:00:48,132
So sine and cosine give
exactly the same values.

19
00:00:48,782 --> 00:00:49,950
Watch the graph.

20
00:00:49,950 --> 00:00:54,148
Every two pi, the same wave is
printed again, like the heartbeat.

21
00:00:54,798 --> 00:00:56,253
Let us write it down.

22
00:00:56,603 --> 00:00:57,503
Step one.

23
00:00:57,418 --> 00:01:01,624
Adding two pi turns P one full
circle, back to the same point.

24
00:01:02,374 --> 00:01:03,410
Step two.

25
00:01:03,410 --> 00:01:09,645
So sine of two pi plus x equals sine
x, and cosine of two pi plus x equals

26
00:01:09,645 --> 00:01:10,682
cosine x.

27
00:01:11,432 --> 00:01:12,431
Step three.

28
00:01:12,431 --> 00:01:14,559
Add two pi again and again.

29
00:01:14,559 --> 00:01:18,945
For any integer n, sine of
two n pi plus x equals sine x.

30
00:01:18,945 --> 00:01:20,953
The same holds for cosine.

31
00:01:21,703 --> 00:01:22,603
Step four.

32
00:01:22,595 --> 00:01:27,594
The smallest positive gap after which
the values repeat is called the period.

33
00:01:27,594 --> 00:01:30,657
For sine and cosine, the period is two pi.

34
00:01:31,407 --> 00:01:33,406
Tangent repeats even sooner.

35
00:01:33,406 --> 00:01:34,824
Its period is pi.

36
00:01:35,224 --> 00:01:38,095
Half a turn sends P to the opposite point.

37
00:01:38,095 --> 00:01:42,844
Sine and cosine both flip their signs,
so their ratio, tangent, stays the

38
00:01:42,844 --> 00:01:43,744
same.

39
00:01:44,050 --> 00:01:45,154
Side by side.

40
00:01:45,154 --> 00:01:48,945
Sine and cosine need a full
turn, two pi, to repeat.

41
00:01:49,345 --> 00:01:51,890
Tangent needs only half a turn, pi.

42
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:54,385
So its graph repeats twice as often.

43
00:01:54,985 --> 00:01:56,072
Now you try.

44
00:01:56,072 --> 00:02:01,401
Is sine of seven hundred and fifty degrees
the same as sine of thirty degrees?

45
00:02:01,401 --> 00:02:02,751
Pause and decide.

46
00:02:05,951 --> 00:02:06,851
Yes!

47
00:02:06,465 --> 00:02:10,393
Seven fifty is two full turns,
seven twenty, plus thirty.

48
00:02:10,393 --> 00:02:14,130
So sine seven fifty is sine
thirty, which is one half.

49
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:15,970
Now symmetry.

50
00:02:15,970 --> 00:02:20,159
Turn P by minus x, clockwise
instead of anticlockwise.

51
00:02:20,559 --> 00:02:24,125
The new point is the mirror
image of P in the x axis.

52
00:02:24,125 --> 00:02:27,002
Same side to side
position, opposite height.

53
00:02:27,402 --> 00:02:33,184
So cosine of minus x equals cosine
x, and sine of minus x equals minus

54
00:02:33,184 --> 00:02:34,098
sine x.

55
00:02:34,748 --> 00:02:38,525
On the graph, cosine is a
mirror image about the y axis.

56
00:02:38,525 --> 00:02:42,399
Fold the paper along the y
axis, and the two halves match.

57
00:02:43,049 --> 00:02:44,992
Sine has a different symmetry.

58
00:02:44,992 --> 00:02:49,343
Spin its graph half a turn about
the origin, and it lands on itself.

59
00:02:49,993 --> 00:02:52,518
A function like cosine is called even.

60
00:02:52,518 --> 00:02:54,896
A function like sine is called odd.

61
00:02:55,496 --> 00:02:56,692
Remember this.

62
00:02:56,692 --> 00:02:59,455
Sine and cosine repeat every two pi.

63
00:02:59,455 --> 00:03:01,401
Tangent repeats every pi.

64
00:03:02,101 --> 00:03:03,001
Quick one.

65
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,370
Is cosine of minus sixty degrees
plus a half, or minus a half?

66
00:03:07,370 --> 00:03:08,640
Pause and think.

67
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:13,066
Plus a half!

68
00:03:13,066 --> 00:03:18,958
Cosine of minus x equals cosine x, so
cosine of minus sixty is cosine sixty,

69
00:03:18,958 --> 00:03:19,934
one half.

70
00:03:20,584 --> 00:03:22,319
Now, one worked example.

71
00:03:22,669 --> 00:03:26,846
Find the value of sine of minus
one thousand and fifty degrees.

72
00:03:27,346 --> 00:03:28,301
Step one.

73
00:03:28,301 --> 00:03:29,501
Sine is odd.

74
00:03:29,501 --> 00:03:34,963
So sine of minus one thousand fifty
is minus sine of one thousand fifty.

75
00:03:35,713 --> 00:03:36,613
Step two.

76
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:41,518
One thousand fifty is three full turns,
one thousand eighty, minus thirty.

77
00:03:41,518 --> 00:03:43,038
Drop the full turns.

78
00:03:43,038 --> 00:03:45,089
We get sine of minus thirty.

79
00:03:45,839 --> 00:03:46,798
Step three.

80
00:03:46,798 --> 00:03:51,466
Sine of minus thirty is minus sine
thirty, which is minus one half.

81
00:03:52,216 --> 00:03:54,918
So the answer is minus, minus one half.

82
00:03:54,918 --> 00:03:56,521
That is plus one half.

83
00:03:57,171 --> 00:03:58,637
Now, the exam trap.

84
00:03:58,987 --> 00:04:03,883
Many students write: sine of minus
x equals sine x, just like cosine.

85
00:04:04,283 --> 00:04:06,183
But only cosine is even.

86
00:04:06,183 --> 00:04:07,181
Sine flips.

87
00:04:07,181 --> 00:04:11,990
Sine of minus thirty degrees is
minus one half, not plus one half.

88
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:14,054
Where is this asked?

89
00:04:14,054 --> 00:04:18,459
As questions on the values of large
or negative angles, and on the period

90
00:04:18,459 --> 00:04:19,532
of a function.

91
00:04:20,132 --> 00:04:21,348
Exam level now.

92
00:04:21,348 --> 00:04:23,728
Find the value of this expression.

93
00:04:23,728 --> 00:04:24,995
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:30,395 --> 00:04:31,623
The answer is B.

95
00:04:31,623 --> 00:04:32,523
One.

96
00:04:32,609 --> 00:04:36,585
Sine is odd, and seven fifty
is seven twenty plus thirty.

97
00:04:36,585 --> 00:04:39,778
So sine of minus seven
fifty is minus a half.

98
00:04:40,278 --> 00:04:44,327
Cosine is even, and four twenty
is three sixty plus sixty.

99
00:04:44,327 --> 00:04:47,588
So cosine of minus four
twenty is plus a half.

100
00:04:48,088 --> 00:04:50,809
Tangent repeats every one eighty degrees.

101
00:04:50,809 --> 00:04:54,909
Eleven twenty five is six times
one eighty, plus forty five.

102
00:04:54,909 --> 00:04:57,762
So it is tan forty five, which is one.

103
00:04:58,362 --> 00:05:00,856
Minus a half, plus a half, plus one.

104
00:05:00,856 --> 00:05:02,081
The total is one.

105
00:05:02,581 --> 00:05:04,042
Here is your recap.

106
00:05:04,042 --> 00:05:05,544
Screenshot this card.

107
00:05:08,744 --> 00:05:11,743
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

108
00:05:12,143 --> 00:05:13,043
One.

109
00:05:12,649 --> 00:05:14,744
What is the period of tangent?

110
00:05:15,644 --> 00:05:16,544
Two.

111
00:05:16,236 --> 00:05:19,188
Is sine of minus x equal to sine x?

112
00:05:20,088 --> 00:05:20,988
Three.

113
00:05:20,846 --> 00:05:23,790
Simplify cosine of x plus four pi.

114
00:05:24,890 --> 00:05:29,335
Next time: three different looking
expressions always give the same number.

115
00:05:29,335 --> 00:05:31,576
We meet the trigonometric identities.
